"── 115_winui_filein.mst — WG6c-3, finished: File In and Add to World ────
 The two verbs WG6c-3 named and could not build. Its own note recorded why:
 File In's contract is *a fresh world, then your file*, and `win_gui` booted
 its primary once and held it for the process lifetime. WG7-3 built the
 restart; `WinFile` (113) built the write; this is the ten lines between them.

 THE CONTRACT, and it is the whole point rather than a detail: File In does
 not evaluate your buffer into the CURRENT world. It restarts the primary from
 its clean boot state and loads your file on top — so the same file filed in
 twice behaves identically both times, and a class you renamed does not leave
 its old self behind. `68_cocoaeditor.mst` states it the same way, and a
 weaker File In that merely evaluated the buffer would be a different verb
 wearing the same name.

 THE PATH IS DERIVED, NOT CARRIED. The guest writes to
 `<temp>/macvm-editor-filein.mst` and the host restarts on the same path,
 computed its own way (`GetTempPathW` here, `std::env::temp_dir()` there —
 both read TMP/TEMP). Nothing marshals a string through a private message's
 `wParam`, and the coupling is one filename named in both files.

 WHY A PRIVATE MESSAGE. The guest cannot restart the primary — it IS one of
 the two VMs involved — so what crosses is a REQUEST. `WM_APP_FILEIN` is
 recorded by the trampoline and acted on by the pump between dispatches,
 exactly as `WM_APP_DRAIN` is, and for a sharper version of the same reason:
 the work is *join the primary's thread*, which a wndproc must never do."

Object subclass: WinShell [

    WinShell class >> fileInPath ^ <String> [
        "One filename, named identically in `win_gui/src/main.rs`'s
         `filein_scratch_path`. If one moves, both move."
        ^self tempDirectory , 'macvm-editor-filein.mst'
    ]

    WinShell class >> fileIn [
        "Write the buffer, then ask the host for a fresh world with it loaded.
         WRITE FIRST and CHECK: a silent write failure would restart into a
         world without the user's code and look exactly like a file-in that
         did nothing at all."
        | text |
        text := self editorText.
        (text isNil or: [ text isEmpty ]) ifTrue: [
            ^self appendTranscript: 'editor: nothing to file in' ].
        (WinFile spit: self fileInPath contents: text) ifFalse: [
            ^self appendTranscript: 'editor: cannot write ' , self fileInPath
                , ' — ' , WinFile lastError ].
        self appendTranscript: 'editor: file-in — fresh world, then ' , self fileInPath.
        self requestFileIn.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> requestFileIn ^ <Boolean> [
        "POSTED, not sent. A SendMessage would run the restart inside this VM
         entry — the door's busy guard would decline it, and if it did not,
         we would be joining the primary's thread from inside a call the
         primary might be waiting on."
        "`hwndValue`, not `hwnd`: the latter answers a WinRef — a live handle
         OBJECT — and the FFI marshals integers. Passing the WinRef fails as
         `does not understand >=` somewhere inside the marshalling, which is a
         long way from the mistake."
        self isOpen ifFalse: [ ^false ].
        ^WinApi asBool: (WinApi
            postMessage: self hwndValue
            message: (16r8000 + 8)
            wParam: 0
            lParam: 0)
    ]

    WinShell class >> addToWorld [
        "Graduate the buffer INTO THE WORLD: `world/user_<name>.mst` plus a
         line in `world.list`, so every FUTURE fresh world contains it — File
         In's included.

         THE NAME COMES FROM THE CODE, not from a dialog: the first class the
         buffer defines. A file-in scratch buffer with no class in it has
         nothing to graduate, and saying so beats writing `user_nil.mst`.

         Re-adding the same buffer OVERWRITES its file and does not duplicate
         its `world.list` line — which is what makes this idempotent, and what
         lets it be the ordinary way to keep working on the same class."
        | text name path |
        text := self editorText.
        (text isNil or: [ text isEmpty ]) ifTrue: [
            ^self appendTranscript: 'editor: nothing to add to the world' ].
        name := self firstClassNameIn: text.
        name isNil ifTrue: [
            ^self appendTranscript:
                'editor: add-to-world needs a class definition in the buffer' ].
        path := 'world\user_' , name , '.mst'.
        (WinFile spit: path contents: text) ifFalse: [
            ^self appendTranscript: 'editor: cannot write ' , path
                , ' — ' , WinFile lastError ].
        self appendTranscript: 'editor: added ' , path
            , ' — it is in every future fresh world once world.list names it'.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> firstClassNameIn: aString <String> ^ <Object> [
        "The name in `<Something> subclass: Name [`, or nil. Deliberately the
         same shape `flows::parse_mst_source` looks for, so a buffer this
         accepts is one the image writer accepts too — two parsers that
         disagreed about what a class definition IS would be a very confusing
         pair of error messages.

         PURE over a String, so it is checkable with no file and no window."
        | i n start |
        i := self indexOf: 'subclass:' in: aString.
        i = 0 ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        n := i + 9.
        [ n <= aString size and: [ (aString at: n) = $  ] ] whileTrue: [ n := n + 1 ].
        start := n.
        [ n <= aString size and: [ ((aString at: n) isLetter)
            or: [ (aString at: n) isDigit or: [ (aString at: n) = $_ ] ] ] ]
                whileTrue: [ n := n + 1 ].
        n = start ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^aString copyFrom: start to: n - 1
    ]

    WinShell class >> indexOf: needle <String> in: hay <String> ^ <Integer> [
        | limit |
        needle isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        limit := hay size - needle size + 1.
        1 to: limit do: [ :i |
            | ok |
            ok := true.
            1 to: needle size do: [ :j |
                (hay at: i + j - 1) = (needle at: j) ifFalse: [ ok := false ] ].
            ok ifTrue: [ ^i ] ].
        ^0
    ]
]
